# 1.) They are polymorphic methods, having the same name and called the same way, but they are overridden in each child class so they behave differently depending on the character or item.

# 2.) This activity shows polymorphism because a specific command has different outputs depending on the objects that use it. An example is when a mage casts a powerful spell as their special move while a warrior swings a mighty sword. This shows that all characters have special moves but different characters have different special moves.

 # 3.) Yes. For example, the use function on a healing potion gives you more HP, but using the same use function on a speed potion instead makes you faster. 
